Nobody memorizes every single flag for every Linux tool. Instead, Linux has built-in reference manuals directly in your terminal.

A. The Quick Reference: --help
When to use it: When you just forgot the exact letter for a flag (e.g., "Is it -a or -A?").

Behavior: It dumps a quick summary directly into your terminal screen and immediately returns you to the prompt so you can copy/paste or glance at syntax.

Bash
ls --help
B. The Complete Manual: man (The Deep Dive)
When to use it: When you encounter a brand-new tool and need to understand the full syntax, return codes, edge cases, and examples.

Behavior: It opens an interactive text reader (using less under the hood) taking over your entire screen.

Bash
man ls
